%I A397807 #32 Sep 09 2026 16:20:18
%S A397807 0,0,1,3,5,20,53,130
%N A397807 Number of iterations of Gaston's algorithm required for every list of n distinct real numbers in [0, 1) to be either sorted or to never become sorted.
%C A397807 We define Gaston's algorithm as in A397806.
%C A397807 Let L = [x_1, x_2, ..., x_n] be a list of size n of distinct real numbers in [0, 1).
%C A397807 Gaston's algorithm creates a new list containing the elements of L.
%C A397807 First, the first element of L is placed in the resulting list.
%C A397807 Then, every term x_i of L is inserted one by one in the resulting list in position k, such as (k - 1)/i <= x_i < k/i.
%C A397807 a(n) is the number of iterations of Gaston's algorithm required for every n-sized list to be either sorted or to never become sorted.
%C A397807 Gaston's algorithm was introduced in the eighth problem of the 2026 edition of the TFJM^2 (French Tournament of Young Mathematicians) (see links).
%C A397807 Since Gaston's algorithm transforms a list L of size n into one of its permutations, Gaston's algorithm can be iterated at most n! - 1 times before the resulting list has already appeared. Therefore, this is also true before the sorted list is obtained and a(n) <= n! - 1.
%H A397807 Tournoi Français des Jeunes Mathématiciennes et Mathématiciens, <a href="https://tfjm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/index.pdf">Problèmes du 16ème Tournoi Français des Jeunes Mathématiciennes et Mathématiciens</a>, version 2, Jan 08 2026, p. 10.
%H A397807 Romain Malécot, <a href="/A397807/a397807.py.txt">Program in Python for a(n), 0 <= n <= 6</a>
